    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm having a nightmare with our jenkins pipeline. the unit test stage is being super flaky. it fails about 20% of the time but the codebase hasn't changed and the tests pass if i just retry the build. i've checked the basic stuff but i can't figure out what's causing these non-deterministic failures. any ideas on how to isolate the environment or what might be causing this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This sounds like a classic case of flaky tests, possibly due to race conditions, shared resources, or timing issues. To try and isolate it, consider running your test suite in an isolated, clean environment using tools like Docker to mirrored your production setup. Also, investigate any flaky tests that fail randomly by focusing on areas involving external dependencies or asynchronous operations, and replace them with better-designed tests such as using test databases with resetter steps or better mocking strategies. Keep your CI environment consistent to eliminate any external variable causes. Lastly, look into implementing a retry mechanism within Jenkins that only retries certain tests after a mandatory wait, but cap it to avoid an unending loop of potential errors.",
