    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the suggestions. i've already tried docker isolation and that hasn't really helped, the failures still pop up randomly. it's like something is just messing with the execution middle-way through. i've looked at the resource usage and everything seems fine, no obvious spikes in CPU or RAM during the failures. is there anything else i should check regarding how the agents or workers might be interacting with each other or the underlying host?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your situation might indeed involve issues at a more subtle level, such as job queuing, resource contention between concurrent jobs, or silent failures that are not immediately apparent. Investigate Jenkins Master and Agents interaction to ensure there aren't any configuration issues. Look for instances of jobs that could be queuing up in Jenkins and indirectly impacting each other, causing race conditions. Examine if the Jenkins agents have adequate resources and if performance settings such as triggering policies or ThrottleMode need to be adjusted. Review and assess the proper handling of ephemeral data stored between builds, ensuring that this isn't causing inconsistent test results due to improper cleanup. Also, consider looking into build timestamps and ensuring that concurrent jobs do not lead to unexpected interactions. Another good practice is to expand your logging capabilities; more detailed logs might reveal hidden issues. Additionally, use a tool like Chaos Monkey to introduce controlled failures and examine how your system reacts to these failures, providing further insight into the potential causes of your flaky tests.",
      "source": "target",
